<h1>COMMUNITYHELP HUB - PROJECT STUDY GUIDE & CODE WALKTHROUGH</h1>
<p><strong>Project Name:</strong> CommunityHelp Hub (Volunteer & Social Service Platform)</p>
<p><strong>Course / Subject:</strong> Full Stack Development (FSD) Mini Project</p>
<p><strong>Architecture:</strong> Model-View-Controller (MVC) RESTful Architecture</p>
<p><strong>Core Tech Stack:</strong> Node.js, Express.js, MongoDB, Mongoose, HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript (Fetch API), Chart.js, PDFKit</p>

<hr/>

<h2>SECTION 1: HIGH-LEVEL ARCHITECTURE & SYSTEM DESIGN</h2>

<h3>1.1 Problem Statement & Solution</h3>
<p><strong>Problem:</strong> In India, local community organizations and grassroots NGOs frequently face volunteer shortages for short-duration civic drives (e.g., food packaging, remedial teaching, lake cleanup). Simultaneously, university students need verified community service hours for academic credits (such as AICTE/NSS points) but lack a transparent platform to discover opportunities, track hours, and obtain tamper-verifiable certificates.</p>

<p><strong>Solution:</strong> CommunityHelp Hub acts as a centralized, role-based platform that:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Separates user roles into <strong>Volunteers</strong>, <strong>NGOs</strong>, and <strong>Admins</strong>.</li>
  <li>Provides full <strong>CRUD operations</strong> for social-service tasks across 6 mandatory categories.</li>
  <li>Uses <strong>algorithmic skill matching</strong> and <strong>location proximity</strong> to match volunteers with tasks.</li>
  <li>Features an <strong>AI-inspired recommendation engine</strong> scoring tasks out of 100 points.</li>
  <li>Generates <strong>tamper-verifiable PDF certificates</strong> dynamically via PDFKit upon NGO completion approval.</li>
  <li>Visualizes community impact through dynamic <strong>Chart.js</strong> visualizations.</li>
</ul>

<h3>1.2 How the Full-Stack Flow Works</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Client Layer (Browser):</strong> The user opens HTML pages (served statically by Express from <code>public/</code>). When buttons are clicked or forms submitted, vanilla JavaScript functions in <code>public/js/</code> trigger asynchronous <code>fetch()</code> requests to Express API routes.</li>
  <li><strong>Server Layer (Node.js & Express):</strong> Express handles incoming HTTP requests, passes them through authentication middleware (<code>authMiddleware.js</code>) to verify JWT tokens, and routes them to appropriate controller routes in <code>routes/</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Database Layer (MongoDB & Mongoose):</strong> Mongoose models validate and query data from MongoDB running on <code>mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/community_help_hub</code> and return JSON responses to the frontend.</li>
</ol>

<hr/>

<h2>SECTION 2: MONGODB DATABASE SCHEMAS (LINE-BY-LINE)</h2>
<p>The project is structured into 6 interconnected Mongoose collections:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Collection</th>
      <th>Primary Purpose</th>
      <th>Key Relationships</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>users</strong></td>
      <td>Stores authentication credentials and user role</td>
      <td>Linked 1-to-1 with Volunteer or NGO profile</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>volunteers</strong></td>
      <td>Stores volunteer name, city, skills, hours, points</td>
      <td>Foreign key: <code>userId</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>ngos</strong></td>
      <td>Stores organization name, registration ID, phone, city</td>
      <td>Foreign key: <code>userId</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>tasks</strong></td>
      <td>Stores task details, category, required skills, status</td>
      <td>Foreign key: <code>createdBy</code> (ref: NGO)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>applications</strong></td>
      <td>Tracks volunteer task applications and status lifecycle</td>
      <td>Foreign keys: <code>taskId</code>, <code>volunteerId</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>certificates</strong></td>
      <td>Stores generated certificate records and unique IDs</td>
      <td>Foreign keys: <code>volunteerId</code>, <code>taskId</code>, <code>ngoId</code></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>2.1 User Model (<code>models/User.js</code>)</h3>
<p>Handles credentials and password encryption using <code>bcryptjs</code>:</p>
<div class="code-block">
const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  email: { type: String, required: true, unique: true, lowercase: true, trim: true },
  password: { type: String, required: true, minlength: 6 },
  role: { type: String, enum: ['volunteer', 'ngo', 'admin'], default: 'volunteer' },
  createdAt: { type: Date, default: Date.now }
});

// Pre-save hook: Hashes password before saving to MongoDB
userSchema.pre('save', async function (next) {
  if (!this.isModified('password')) return next();
  const salt = await bcrypt.genSalt(10);
  this.password = await bcrypt.hash(this.password, salt);
  next();
});

// Helper method: Compares user entered password with hashed password
userSchema.methods.comparePassword = async function (enteredPassword) {
  return await bcrypt.compare(enteredPassword, this.password);
};
</div>
<p><strong>Viva Explanation:</strong> "Sir/Ma'am, we never store plain-text passwords. We use <code>bcryptjs</code> with a salt round of 10. The <code>pre('save')</code> middleware runs automatically before any user document is written to MongoDB. The <code>comparePassword</code> method allows secure verification during login."</p>

<h3>2.2 Task Model (<code>models/Task.js</code>)</h3>
<p>Strictly enforces the 6 required categories and status tracking:</p>
<div class="code-block">
const taskSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  title: { type: String, required: true, trim: true },
  description: { type: String, required: true, trim: true },
  category: {
    type: String,
    required: true,
    enum: [
      'Education',
      'Food distribution',
      'Environment',
      'Elder support',
      'Disaster support',
      'Community service'
    ]
  },
  requiredSkills: { type: [String], default: [] },
  location: { type: String, required: true },
  city: { type: String, required: true },
  dateTime: { type: Date, required: true },
  hours: { type: Number, required: true, default: 3 },
  volunteersRequired: { type: Number, required: true, min: 1 },
  volunteersAssigned: { type: Number, default: 0 },
  status: {
    type: String,
    enum: ['Open', 'In Progress', 'Completed', 'Cancelled'],
    default: 'Open'
  },
  createdBy: { type: mongoose.Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'NGO', required: true }
});
</div>

<h3>2.3 Application Model (<code>models/Application.js</code>)</h3>
<p>Tracks volunteer assignment lifecycle with a compound unique index to prevent duplicate applications:</p>
<div class="code-block">
const applicationSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  taskId: { type: mongoose.Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'Task', required: true },
  volunteerId: { type: mongoose.Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'Volunteer', required: true },
  status: {
    type: String,
    enum: ['Applied', 'Assigned', 'Completed', 'Rejected'],
    default: 'Applied'
  },
  notes: { type: String, default: '' },
  appliedAt: { type: Date, default: Date.now },
  completedAt: { type: Date }
});

// Prevents a volunteer from applying to the same task multiple times
applicationSchema.index({ taskId: 1, volunteerId: 1 }, { unique: true });
</div>

<hr/>

<h2>SECTION 3: AUTHENTICATION & SECURITY (JWT)</h2>

<h3>3.1 How Login & Token Verification Work</h3>
<ol>
  <li>The user submits their email and password to <code>POST /api/auth/login</code>.</li>
  <li>The backend verifies the credentials using <code>user.comparePassword()</code>.</li>
  <li>If valid, the server signs a JSON Web Token using <code>jwt.sign({ userId, role }, JWT_SECRET, { expiresIn: '7d' })</code>.</li>
  <li>The client receives the token and stores it in <code>localStorage.setItem('token', token)</code>.</li>
  <li>On every subsequent request, the frontend fetch wrapper (<code>public/js/api.js</code>) attaches the header: <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>.</li>
</ol>

<h3>3.2 Protecting Routes (<code>middleware/authMiddleware.js</code>)</h3>
<div class="code-block">
const protect = async (req, res, next) => {
  let token;
  if (req.headers.authorization && req.headers.authorization.startsWith('Bearer')) {
    try {
      token = req.headers.authorization.split(' ')[1];
      const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
      const user = await User.findById(decoded.userId).select('-password');
      req.user = { userId: user._id, email: user.email, role: user.role };

      // Attach profile ID based on role
      if (user.role === 'volunteer') {
        const vol = await Volunteer.findOne({ userId: user._id });
        if (vol) req.user.volunteerId = vol._id;
      } else if (user.role === 'ngo') {
        const ngo = await NGO.findOne({ userId: user._id });
        if (ngo) req.user.ngoId = ngo._id;
      }
      next();
    } catch (error) {
      return res.status(401).json({ success: false, message: 'Invalid or expired token' });
    }
  }
};
</div>

<hr/>

<h2>SECTION 4: THE 6 ADVANCED FEATURES (HOW THE CODE WORKS)</h2>

<h3>Feature 1: Skill-Based Volunteer Matching</h3>
<p><strong>Endpoint:</strong> <code>GET /api/tasks/matching/skills</code> (in <code>routes/taskRoutes.js</code>)</p>
<p><strong>How the Algorithm Works:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Finds the logged-in volunteer's registered skills array (e.g. <code>['Teaching', 'English', 'First Aid']</code>).</li>
  <li>Fetches all active community tasks from MongoDB.</li>
  <li>For each task, counts how many required skills match the volunteer's skills (common skills intersection).</li>
  <li>Calculates the match percentage: <code>(commonSkills / taskSkills.length) * 100</code>.</li>
  <li>Sorts tasks descending by match score, displaying a dynamic badge: <code>[100% SKILL MATCH]</code>.</li>
</ol>
<div class="code-block">
let commonSkills = 0;
taskSkills.forEach((skill) => {
  if (volunteerSkills.includes(skill)) commonSkills++;
});

let matchScore = 0;
if (taskSkills.length > 0) {
  matchScore = Math.round((commonSkills / taskSkills.length) * 100);
} else {
  matchScore = 50; // Neutral score
}
</div>

<h3>Feature 2: Location-Based Matching</h3>
<p><strong>Endpoint:</strong> <code>GET /api/tasks/matching/location</code></p>
<p><strong>How it Works:</strong> Queries tasks matching the volunteer's city using a case-insensitive regular expression: <code>city: new RegExp(`^${volunteer.city}$`, 'i')</code>. Tasks in the volunteer's home metropolitan area are prioritized at the top.</p>

<h3>Feature 3: AI Task Recommendation Engine</h3>
<p><strong>Endpoint:</strong> <code>GET /api/tasks/recommendations/ai</code></p>
<p><strong>How the Algorithm Works:</strong> Uses a 4-factor multi-criteria scoring algorithm calculating a composite score out of 100 points:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Skill Match Weight (Up to 40 Points):</strong> <code>(matchedSkills / totalSkills) * 40</code></li>
  <li><strong>Location Proximity (Up to 30 Points):</strong> 30 points if the task is located in the volunteer's registered city.</li>
  <li><strong>Historical Category Interest (Up to 20 Points):</strong> 20 points if the volunteer previously completed tasks in this category.</li>
  <li><strong>Urgent Need & Open Capacity (10 Points):</strong> 10 points if the task has open volunteer spots.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Feature 4: Volunteer Ranking & Leaderboard</h3>
<p><strong>Endpoint:</strong> <code>GET /api/dashboard/rankings</code></p>
<p><strong>Scoring Math:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Each completed task awards <strong>10 base points</strong>.</li>
  <li>Each hour of verified service awards <strong>2 points per hour</strong>.</li>
  <li><code>rankingPoints = completedTasksCount * 10 + totalHours * 2</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Recognition Tiers:</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>0 to 39 Points: <strong>Bronze Tier</strong></li>
      <li>40 to 79 Points: <strong>Silver Tier</strong></li>
      <li>80+ Points: <strong>Gold Champion</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>Feature 5: Automated PDF Certificate Generation (PDFKit)</h3>
<p><strong>Endpoint:</strong> <code>GET /api/certificates/download/:id</code> (in <code>routes/certificateRoutes.js</code>)</p>
<p><strong>How it Works:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Finds the certificate record in MongoDB and populates volunteer name, task details, hours, and NGO name.</li>
  <li>Initializes a landscape A4 PDF document: <code>new PDFDocument({ layout: 'landscape', size: 'A4' })</code>.</li>
  <li>Sets HTTP response headers: <code>Content-Type: application/pdf</code> and <code>Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="Certificate-...pdf"</code>.</li>
  <li>Pipes the document stream directly to the Express response: <code>doc.pipe(res)</code>.</li>
  <li>Draws vector borders, headers, student details, hours, issue date, and a unique certificate ID.</li>
  <li>Calls <code>doc.end()</code> to finalize and stream the file.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Feature 6: Impact Dashboard with Chart.js</h3>
<p><strong>File:</strong> <code>public/impact.html</code> and <code>public/js/dashboard.js</code></p>
<p>Uses MongoDB aggregation pipelines (<code>Task.aggregate([ { $group: { _id: '$category', count: { $sum: 1 } } } ])</code>) to group tasks by category and city. Renders an interactive Chart.js doughnut chart and bar chart with distinct category colors.</p>

<hr/>

