<h2>SECTION 6: 15 VIVA QUESTIONS & ANSWERS FOR YOUR EXAM</h2>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q1: What is the primary architecture of your project?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "Sir/Ma'am, it follows a 3-tier RESTful Client-Server architecture. The presentation layer consists of responsive HTML5, CSS3, and JavaScript calling REST API endpoints via asynchronous Fetch. The application layer is built on Node.js with Express.js handling routing and middleware. The database layer uses MongoDB with Mongoose for schema modeling."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q2: Why did you use MongoDB instead of a relational database like MySQL?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "MongoDB is a document-oriented NoSQL database that stores data in JSON-like BSON documents. Social service tasks have dynamic, semi-structured attributes such as arrays of required skills and variable location requirements. MongoDB handles arrays naturally without requiring complex many-to-many junction tables."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q3: How is authentication handled securely?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "We use JSON Web Tokens (JWT) and Bcrypt. Passwords are hashed using salted hashing before saving to MongoDB. When a user logs in, the server generates a signed JWT containing their user ID and role, which is stored in localStorage and sent in the Authorization header of subsequent requests."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q4: How do you prevent unauthorized users from creating tasks?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "In <code>middleware/authMiddleware.js</code>, we have the <code>requireNGO</code> middleware. When a request hits <code>POST /api/tasks</code>, Express first verifies the JWT token. If <code>req.user.role !== 'ngo'</code>, the request is immediately rejected with HTTP 403 Forbidden."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q5: What are the 6 mandatory task categories in your platform?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "The 6 categories are Education, Food distribution, Environment, Elder support, Disaster support, and Community service. These are strictly enforced using Mongoose schema validation in <code>models/Task.js</code>."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q6: How does the skill-based matching algorithm work?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "In <code>routes/taskRoutes.js</code>, the <code>/matching/skills</code> route compares the volunteer's registered skills array with the task's required skills array. It computes the intersection count of common skills and calculates a percentage match score: <code>(commonSkills / taskSkills.length) * 100</code>."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q7: How does your AI task recommendation engine score opportunities?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "In <code>GET /api/tasks/recommendations/ai</code>, we implement a multi-criteria heuristic scoring algorithm out of 100 points: up to 40 points for skill overlap, 30 points for city proximity, 20 points for historical interest in that category, and 10 points for available capacity."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q8: How does the certificate generation feature work?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "When an NGO marks a volunteer's assignment as 'Completed', a Certificate record with a unique ID is created. When the volunteer clicks 'Download PDF', our Express endpoint uses <code>pdfkit</code> to dynamically draw a vector Certificate of Appreciation in memory and streams it directly to the browser with <code>Content-Type: application/pdf</code>."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q9: How is the volunteer ranking leaderboard computed?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "Volunteers earn 10 points per completed task plus 2 points for every verified hour of service. The <code>/api/dashboard/rankings</code> endpoint queries volunteers sorted descending by <code>rankingPoints</code> and classifies them into Bronze, Silver, and Gold tiers."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q10: How do you prevent duplicate applications by the same volunteer?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "In <code>models/Application.js</code>, we defined a compound unique index: <code>applicationSchema.index({ taskId: 1, volunteerId: 1 }, { unique: true })</code>. If a volunteer attempts to apply twice, MongoDB enforces uniqueness and rejects the duplicate record."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q11: How is data visualized on the Impact Dashboard?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "We use <code>Chart.js</code> on the frontend. The backend runs a MongoDB aggregation pipeline using <code>$group</code> to aggregate task counts by category and city. The frontend feeds this aggregated data into a Chart.js doughnut chart and bar chart."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q12: What happens when an NGO marks an assignment as 'Completed'?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "Three actions occur automatically: 1) The volunteer's <code>completedTasksCount</code> and <code>totalHours</code> are incremented, 2) Their <code>rankingPoints</code> are updated, and 3) An official <code>Certificate</code> document with a unique ID is created in MongoDB."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q13: Why did you not use a frontend framework like React or Angular?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "Using pure HTML5, CSS3, and vanilla JavaScript demonstrates core proficiency in asynchronous DOM manipulation, fetch requests, and state synchronization without the overhead of build tools or external compilation dependencies."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q14: How are passwords compared during login if they are hashed?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "Bcrypt uses a one-way mathematical hashing algorithm. We cannot decrypt a hash. Instead, <code>bcrypt.compare()</code> takes the plain-text password entered at login, applies the same salt, hashes it, and checks if the resulting hash matches the hash stored in MongoDB."</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <p><strong>Q15: What npm packages are used and why?</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> "<code>express</code> for HTTP routing, <code>mongoose</code> for MongoDB modeling, <code>bcryptjs</code> for password hashing, <code>jsonwebtoken</code> for token authorization, <code>dotenv</code> for environment variables, <code>cors</code> for cross-origin requests, and <code>pdfkit</code> for server-side PDF generation."</p>
</div>

